From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 20:58:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 182/185] Sega CD: I: becomes a file inside Sega's volume

Our FAT12 went over the whole of the backup memory, footer and all, so a
cartridge could carry our files or a game's saves and not both. Now the
volume stays Sega's and our filesystem is one file in it, AROSDISK,
sitting in the console's own Backup RAM Manager beside anything else.

Sector i is bytes [i*512, i*512+512) of that file, reached at
a0out + 1 + 2*i -- the odd-byte mapping bram_rd() has always used, and
the offset measured rather than assumed. Reads and writes both go
straight through, because a file's data takes a write in place.

bvol_rd/bvol_wr/bvol_sectors are the one seam: the formatter and the
block device go through them and never know which home they have, so an
old whole-memory volume keeps working exactly as it did. bram_probe
prefers a Sega volume with our file in it, falls back to the FAT12
signature, and otherwise leaves the memory alone -- a Sega volume with
no AROSDISK is somebody's saves, and the moment to take 7 KB of it is
when they ask.

Which is segacd_bram_rw(), because FORMATI.PRG formats by writing
sectors through Rwabs and there is no other entry point to hook. On a
write, once per session, to a Sega volume with no file of ours: create
it, then let the sectors land inside. On a read, never.

bfs_create allocates with BRMWRITE pointed at EmuTOS's own code for its
source. That is not a trick for its own sake: BRMWRITE copies size*64
bytes and there is nowhere here to put 7680 of them, and every byte it
copies is overwritten a moment later through the pointer. The call is
being used to allocate. Any readable bytes would do.

Measured end to end:

    0040  60 38 45 6d 75 54 4f 53 ...   our boot sector, at byte 64
    1fc0  5f x11 00 00 00 00 40         Sega's footer, untouched
    1fd0  00 05 x4  00 01 x4            5 free, 1 file
    1fe0  SEGA_CD_ROM. RAM_CARTRIDGE___

and on the next boot the manager answers 03020501 -- Sega formatted,
5 blocks free, one file. A plain boot with no formatting leaves such a
volume byte for byte alone; all 8128 bytes before the footer still zero.

Two corrections to what was written down yesterday. The eight bytes at
end-0x28 are the file count, four times over, not padding -- they read
00 01 x4 with our file present, and Genesis Plus GX's template only ever
shows zeros there because it only ever writes an empty volume. And the
directory stores the filename in the manager's own encoding, not ASCII,
so grepping an image for AROSDISK says it is missing when it is there.

Owned: rewriting the formatter, I replaced a range of the file I had
assumed held only bram_format, and the whole Backup RAM manager block
was inside it. Recovered from HEAD and checked symbol by symbol against
it -- nothing else went with it.

Desktop 26213da8979c5fbb on disc and cartridge.
